f_auto_filters: add deinterlace=ivtc, an inverse telecine mode

DVD film is 23.976 fps telecined to 29.97 interlaced: three of every five
frames are whole film frames split across two fields, two are mixes of
neighbouring film frames. Every deinterlace mode treats it as video, so
the rebuilt frames keep jagged outlines in animation and motion judders,
and the stream is presented at 59.94 fields per second instead of the
23.976 frames it carries (edde746/plezy#2710). Running fieldmatch and
decimate through --vf instead does not compose with deinterlace=auto: the
auto deinterlacer sits before the user filters and feeds them fields.

deinterlace=ivtc behaves like auto, except that interlaced video at
29.97 or 30 fps gets inverse telecine in place of the deinterlacer:
fieldmatch rebuilds each film frame from its two fields, bwdif
deinterlaces only the frames left combed (fades, video-shot inserts), and
decimate drops the repeated frame in each cycle of five. Once engaged it
stays on for the rest of the stream, because hard-telecined streams often
flag only the combed frames of each cycle as interlaced. Input is
converted to 8-bit planar YUV first (fieldmatch takes nothing else),
downloading hardware frames, so libavfilter needs no scale filter. When
the chain cannot be built the frame is deinterlaced as with auto.

deinterlace-active stays no while inverse telecine runs, and the new
ivtc-active property says it does, so an embedder matching the display
rate presents four fifths of container-fps rather than twice it.

